WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:10.090
De novo sequencing and assembly is typically
applied to organisms where no reference genome

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:14.590
is available or the available reference is
of poor quality.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:19.500
Genomes that have not been sequenced before
must be assembled via a de novo approach following

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.680
sequencing.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.539
This assembly can then be used for additional
analyses and the basis for future resequencing

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:27.800
projects.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.930
Resequencing is typically performed when a
reference genome sequence is available.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.690
Sequencing reads are aligned back to the reference
to determine the location in the genome the

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.129
specific read best matches.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:45.839
Resequencing is often applied to explore genetic
variation in individuals, families and populations,

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:49.410
particularly with respect to human genetic
disease.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.120
Requirements for sequencing depth in these
studies are governed by the variant type of

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.370
interest, the disease model and the size of
the regions of interest.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.239
Resequencing can reveal single nucleotide
polymorphisms, small insertions or deletions,

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:06.549
structural variants, and copy number variation.

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:12.250
Naturally, the design of a particular study
depends on the biological hypothesis in question,

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.360
and different sequencing strategies are used
for population studies compared with those

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:21.070
for studies of Mendelian disease or of somatic
mutations in cancer.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.930
Furthermore, targeted resequencing approaches
allow a trade-off between sequencing breadth

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.880
and sample numbers: for the same cost, more
samples can be sequenced to the same depth

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.910
but over a smaller genomic region.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:39.340
Here, we discuss the merits of whole-genome
sequencing (WGS) relative to targeted resequencing

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.850
approaches, including WES, in the context
of these different variant types and disease

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.560
models.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.830
High-depth WGS is the 'gold standard' for
DNA resequencing because it can interrogate

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:57.770
all variant types including SNVs, indels,
structural variants and CNVs in both the minority

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:03.610
(1.2%) of the human genome that encodes proteins
and the remaining majority of non-coding sequences.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:09.280
WES is focused on the detection of SNVs and
indels in protein-coding genes and on other

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.110
functional elements such as microRNA sequences;
consequently, it omits regulatory regions

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.079
such as promoters and enhancers.

00:02:18.079 --> 00:02:24.060
Although costs vary depending on the sequence
capture solution, WES can be an order of magnitude

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.889
less expensive than WGS to achieve an approximately
equivalent breadth of coverage of protein-coding

00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:31.329
exons.

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:35.430
These reduced costs offer the potential to
greatly increase sample numbers, which is

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.970
a key factor for many studies.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:43.450
However, WES has various limitations that
are discussed below.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.129
Early genome resequencing studies focused
specifically on the two most common classes

00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:52.709
of sequence variation, which are SNVs and
small indels.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:57.099
The first human genome that was sequenced
using Illumina short-read technology showed

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:03.060
that, although almost all homozygous SNVs
are detected at a 15× average depth, an average

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:09.170
depth of 33× is required to detect the same
proportion of heterozygous SNVs.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:15.519
Consequently, an average depth that exceeds
30× rapidly became the de facto standard.

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:20.409
Although read quality is mostly governed by
sequencing technology, the uniformity of depth

00:03:20.409 --> 00:03:23.489
of coverage can also be affected by sample
preparation.

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:30.840
A GC bias that is introduced during DNA amplification
by PCR has been identified as a major source

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.370
of variation in coverage.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:38.610
Elimination of PCR amplification results in
improved coverage of high GC regions of the

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.860
genome and in fewer duplicate reads.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:48.010
In WES, differences in the hybridization efficiency
of sequence capture probes, which are possibly

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:53.120
again attributable to GC content variation,
can result in target regions that have little

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.569
or no coverage.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:04:00.209
Uniformity of coverage will also be influenced
by repetitive or low-complexity sequences,

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.319
which either restrict bait design or lead
to off-target capture.

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:11.170
Furthermore, unlike WGS, WES still routinely
uses PCR amplification, which must be carefully

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.540
optimized to reduce GC bias.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.340
As a result of increased variation in coverage,
a greater average read depth is required to

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:25.350
achieve the same breadth of coverage as that
from WGS, and an 80× average depth is required

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.640
to cover 89.6–96.8% of target bases.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.170
All WES kits are prone to reference bias,
which arises from capture probes that match

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:41.110
the reference sequence and thus tend to preferentially
enrich the reference allele at heterozygous

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:45.870
sites; such bias can produce false-negative
SNV calls.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:53.810
CNVs can be detected from WGS and WES data
using methods that analyse depth of coverage.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.530
These methods pile up aligned reads against
genomic coordinates, then calculate read counts

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.650
in windows to provide the average depth across
a region.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.060
Copy number changes can then be inferred from
variation in average depth across genomic

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.870
regions.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:15.450
In WGS, reasonable specificity can be obtained
with an average depth of as little as 0.1×.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:21.250
However, sensitivity, break-point detection
and absolute copy number estimation all improve

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.700
with increasing read depth.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.590
Regardless of average read depth, depth-of-coverage
methods are vulnerable to false positives

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:33.310
that are being called owing to local variations
in coverage even after correction for both

00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:39.400
GC bias and 'mappability', and cross-sample
calling is required to reduce this effect.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:45.620
In contrast to the high depth that is required
to accurately call SNVs and indels in individual

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.920
genomes, population genomics studies benefit
from a trade-off between sample numbers and

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.400
sequencing depth, in which many genomes are
sequenced at low depth (for example, 400 samples

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.770
at 4×) and their variants are called jointly
across all samples.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.200
Variant calls on individual low-depth genomes
have a high false-positive rate, but this

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.250
is mitigated by combining information across
samples.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:13.900
This approach provides good power to detect
common variants at a proportion of the sequencing

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.100
cost of deep sequencing.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:23.580
Indeed, even ultra-low-coverage sequencing
(that is, sequencing at 0.1–0.5×) captures

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.690
almost as much common variation (that is,
variants with &gt;1% allele frequency) as single-nucleotide

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.050
polymorphism (SNP) arrays.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:34.800
Conversely, reliable identification of variants
in either highly aneuploid genomes or heterogeneous

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.980
cell populations, such as those from tumours,
requires greater depth of coverage than those

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.310
from normal tissue.

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:47.900
Targeted enrichment and ultra-deep sequencing
(that is, sequencing at 1,000×) of limited

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.840
regions of interest can be used to study clonal
evolution in cancer samples, in which specific

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.310
variants are present in &lt;1% of the cell population.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:02.340
The identification of disease-causing de novo
or recessive variants is often best served

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.690
by sequencing parent–child trios.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:10.300
In this case, it is recommended that the same
depth of sequencing is obtained for each of

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:15.370
the family members in order to minimize false-positive
calls in the proband and false-negative calls

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.020
in the parents.

